    "created_at": "2016-07-28T18:13:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-07-28T18:13:22Z",
    "body": "I don't think @dmethvin's proposal is quite correct. The correspondence between the IDL and content attributes is already well-specified by saying that the IDL attribute reflects the content attribute. The sentence the OP is referring to is, I believe, about the \"ordinal value\" of the list item, which is what is used in rendering.\n\nMy suggestion would be to give a more concrete algorithm for calculating ordinal value. As illustrated by http://jsbin.com/revotubuvo/edit?html,output, this seems a bit strange (for the case where the attribute is omitted/empty/unparseable). But here's a draft\n\nIf the parent element is an ol element, then the li element has an ordinal value. Determine the element's ordinal value as follows:\n- If the value attribute is present, and its value can be parsed as an integer, return the result of that parsing.\n- If there is another li element previous to this one inside the ol, let _previous_ be the ordinal value of that previous li element, and return _previous_ + 1.\n- Otherwise, return 1.\n\nI think this would need to be modified to account for reverse as well.\n\n---\n\nThe more general problem of \"treat as absent\" being unclear would, I think, need to be evaluated on a case-by-case basis. I don't believe \"whenever the spec mentions that when an attribute is being treated as absent, it does not mean the attribute is being treated as completely absent; it is only considered as absent for the purpose of parsing\" is correct...\n",
